---
/* §33 / SPEC §0.1 — what a world load looks like before the world exists.

   Two elements and they have different lifetimes, which is the whole of the
   design:

   - **`.escape` is permanent.** It is the way out, it is in the HTML the
     server sent, and it is the first focusable thing on the page. It is up
     before the entry bundle has arrived, it stays up for the whole load, and
     it is still there once the world is flying — one control in one place,
     never a pair that hand over. §0.1: nobody is trapped, and the control is
     always visible.
   - **`.curtain` is for the load only.** An opaque `--void` layer over the
     document, carrying the honest progress state §0.1 asks for. It fades out
     once the canvas has a frame in it, and `scene.ts` removes it.

   The document underneath is painted and untouched — it is what LCP sees and
   what a crawler reads — it is simply never *looked* at on a world load. It
   is made `inert` rather than hidden for exactly that reason: `inert` takes
   it out of the tab order and the accessibility tree without touching
   `visibility`, which is the one thing that would put the hero's paint in
   question. (It would not, in fact — LCP counts text that is
   `visibility: hidden` — but "would not, in fact" is a worse guarantee than
   not raising the question.)

   Both are rendered on every page and both are display: none until the head
   script writes `data-mode="world"` on the root. With JS off that attribute
   never appears, so this component is invisible to a crawler and to a
   no-script reader, and the page is the document. */
---

<!-- Both of these are children of <body> and outside the document's own
     <main>, which in world mode is behind an opaque canvas and out of the
     accessibility tree anyway. So each has to be its own named landmark,
     for the reason §32's station panel is one: without it axe reports
     `region` on every node here, and the reader's only reachable content
     sits outside every landmark on the page. Measured: two violations in
     the curtain, one once it is flying; zero with these. -->
<nav class="escape" aria-label="Site mode">
  <a href="/?doc" data-escape>Read the document</a>
</nav>

<section class="curtain" aria-label="Loading the world">
  <span class="curtain-mark">theophilusjohn.com</span>

  <div class="curtain-state">
    <div
      class="curtain-bar"
      role="progressbar"
      aria-label="Progress"
      aria-valuemin="0"
      aria-valuemax="100"
      aria-valuenow="0"
      aria-valuetext="0%, starting"
    >
      <i></i>
    </div>
    <p class="curtain-read"><b>0%</b><span>starting</span></p>
  </div>
</section>
